{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues that §11.3 flags are sustained because a single non-discriminating test case (SRC_005) was used to adjudicate two distinct critics, and that repeated failure on that one input is 'evidence of test-design deficiency' rather than critic deficiency. This is a fallacy of relative privation: the mere fact that a test case is non-discriminating (both critics fail) does not establish that the critics are not deficient — it is entirely possible for a degenerate test to expose a genuine deficiency shared by both critics. The argument collapses the distinction between 'the test cannot distinguish critic A from critic B' and 'the test provides no evidence of deficiency at all.' A non-discriminating test that two independent critics both fail on is fully consistent with the audit-ritual's concern that critics share a deficient assumption; SRC_001's causal explanation ('most economical') is asserted, not demonstrated, and the counterfactual — that the test could be degenerate yet still reveal real critic faults — is never ruled out.","premise":"A non-discriminating test case can simultaneously be degenerate (cannot distinguish between two critics) and still expose a genuine deficiency in both critics.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"0c61961a8085","quote":"§11.3 flags are sustained because the two critics were tested on the same artifact (0c61961a8085 / SRC_005) and both were found to have failed, which implies either (a) the critics share a common failure mode or (b) the test case itself is degenerate."}],"counterexample":null}]}